1
00:00:01,600 --> 00:00:06,020
My mum, Evelyn, has always been the one who
remembers everyone's birthday.

2
00:00:06,540 --> 00:00:08,560
Now, small things can interrupt her day.

3
00:00:08,980 --> 00:00:10,540
She loses her place making tea.

4
00:00:11,040 --> 00:00:12,500
Small print is harder to read.

5
00:00:12,860 --> 00:00:15,580
And sometimes, she asks where she left her
glasses again.

6
00:00:19,160 --> 00:00:21,260
She doesn't need another complicated app.

7
00:00:21,600 --> 00:00:24,020
She needs a little context, right when it
matters.

8
00:00:24,440 --> 00:00:25,740
That's why we built Threadline.

9
00:00:26,180 --> 00:00:28,380
Her Vexo band captures the conversation.

10
00:00:28,760 --> 00:00:30,320
A camera adds what's in front of her.

11
00:00:30,620 --> 00:00:33,300
And her saved words help the assistant pick
up the thread.

12
00:00:36,720 --> 00:00:40,200
Here, Evelyn said she wanted to make tea,
then water her plant.

13
00:00:40,520 --> 00:00:41,640
The phone interrupted her.

14
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:44,800
When she asks what she was doing, Threadline
can return to

15
00:00:44,840 --> 00:00:48,020
that plan, show its source, and offer one
next step.

16
00:00:48,360 --> 00:00:50,200
It doesn't pretend the task is finished.

17
00:00:59,560 --> 00:01:01,880
The same assistant adapts to different
needs.

18
00:01:02,300 --> 00:01:03,840
Memory helps recover a plan.

19
00:01:04,280 --> 00:01:05,680
Sight describes what's visible.

20
00:01:06,060 --> 00:01:08,460
Hearing brings speech into large captions.

21
00:01:08,840 --> 00:01:11,840
Mobility keeps the person's request for help
at the center.

22
00:01:16,220 --> 00:01:18,720
The dashboard and memory graph connect those
moments.

23
00:01:19,100 --> 00:01:21,280
A daughter like me can review the context,

24
00:01:21,420 --> 00:01:23,940
instead of starting every conversation from
scratch.

25
00:01:28,360 --> 00:01:30,900
If Mum wants help, Threadline prepares a
message.

26
00:01:31,280 --> 00:01:31,960
She reviews it.

27
00:01:32,220 --> 00:01:33,340
She decides what to share.

28
00:01:33,720 --> 00:01:36,660
A draft is never passed off as a message
already sent.

29
00:01:41,100 --> 00:01:42,480
This is an early prototype.

30
00:01:42,760 --> 00:01:46,960
The examples are illustrative; the interface
and connected reasoning are real.

31
00:01:47,380 --> 00:01:51,420
Our aim is simple: less effort between
needing support and getting it.

32
00:01:54,800 --> 00:01:55,320
Threadline.

33
00:01:55,760 --> 00:01:56,620
A little closer.

34
00:01:57,120 --> 00:01:57,660
Every day.
